I couldn't migrate `src/services/user-service.js` because permission to write files was declined. Nothing on disk changed and I didn't run the tests.

**What I found:**
- `UserRepository` (`src/repositories/user-repository.js`) already has all three methods the service needs: `findActive`, `findById` and `softDelete`.
- `src/api/routes.js:8-9` calls `listActiveUsers` and `findUser` with callbacks. Once the service returns promises, those two routes break unless they're changed too. That's one file beyond what the migration skill covers.

**The changes ready to apply:**

`src/services/user-service.js`:
```js
import { UserRepository } from '../repositories/user-repository.js';

const users = new UserRepository();

export function listActiveUsers() {
  return users.findActive();
}

export function findUser(id) {
  return users.findById(id);
}

export function softDeleteUser(id, when) {
  return users.softDelete(id, when);
}
```

`tests/user-service.test.js`: the two tests become `async` and await the functions:
```js
test('listActiveUsers excludes soft-deleted users', async () => {
  const users = await listActiveUsers();
  assert.equal(users.length, 2);
  assert.ok(users.every((u) => u.deleted_at === null));
});

test('findUser returns null for an unknown id', async () => {
  const user = await findUser(999);
  assert.equal(user, null);
});
```

`src/api/routes.js`: the two user routes convert the promise back to a callback, so the router's own callback style stays the same:
```js
{ method: 'GET', path: '/users', handler: (req, cb) => listActiveUsers().then((r) => cb(null, r), cb) },
{ method: 'GET', path: '/users/:id', handler: (req, cb) => findUser(Number(req.params.id)).then((r) => cb(null, r), cb) },
```

If you allow the writes, I'll apply these and run `node --test tests/user-service.test.js`. If you'd rather handle the router change separately, I can change only the service and the test file, but the two user routes will be broken until then.

